WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
Contact lenses once meant grinding individual pieces of glass by hand. Now the raw polymer

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:11.940
inside each one costs only pennies at scale. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 95.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:18.100
In the 1930s a single pair of glass scleral lenses required hours of custom grinding

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:24.420
on a lathe. The material itself was ordinary glass. Yet the finished product reflected every

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.620
step of bespoke fitting and polishing. Once manufacturers shifted to hydrogel polymers formed

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:35.940
in high volume molds, the cost of the polymer inside each lens dropped to a few pennies.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.900
The lenses themselves became disposable commodities. That gap between raw material and finished price

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:48.800
is the signal worth following. Glass scleral lenses followed the established path of precision

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:54.640
optics. An optician or laboratory started with a blank of glass and removed material until

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.080
the curvature matched the patient's eye. This was done exactly as spectacle lenses had been made

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:07.300
for centuries. Each lens was therefore a one-off part. Its price included skilled labor, multiple grinding

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:13.940
stages, polishing, and repeated trial fittings. The assumption baked into the process was that optical

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:19.400
accuracy on the eye demanded the same rigid tolerances and hand finishing used for telescopes

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:25.960
or cameras. Because the method copied existing craft practice, no one asked whether the eye actually

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:31.800
required a hard custom ground shell. No one asked whether a different material could conform without

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.820
grinding. The result was a finished lens whose costs sat far above the commodity price of the glass

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:42.800
blank. The difference was absorbed in process steps that had simply never been questioned.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.380
The convention persisted because the eye was treated as another optical surface that needed the

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:54.040
same subtractive shaping used for microscopes or camera objectives. An optician would measure

00:01:54.040 --> 00:02:00.100
the cornea and select a blank whose base curve was close. Then came successive sessions removing glass

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:06.620
in finer and finer abrasives until the fits stopped causing pressure points. Each iteration risked

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:12.120
cracking the blank or introducing surface irregularities that scattered light. The process carried

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:18.020
built in scrap and rework. Patients paid for the cumulative time of the optician, the specialized

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:24.300
lathes, the polishing compounds, and the multiple office visits required to verify comfort and vision.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:31.960
The redesign began by asking what the eye actually needs. It needs a thin, oxygen permeable layer that

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:38.120
sits on the cornea without causing damage. Hydrogel polymers satisfy that requirement because they are

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:44.800
soft enough to conform and contain enough water to allow oxygen transmission. Once the material choice

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.820
was fixed, the next question was how to shape it at the lowest cost. Instead of starting with a solid

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:57.340
blank and removing material, manufacturers polymerize the hydrogel directly inside a mold whose inner

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:03.420
surface already carried the final optical curve. The magic wand floor is the cost of the liquid

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:09.260
monomers and cross-linkers that become the finished lens. Those commodity chemicals, bought in bulk,

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:14.220
amount to only a few pennies per lens once the polymerization line runs continuously.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:20.420
The old glass design therefore carried an idiot index set by the ratio of its high finished

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:26.900
price to the low cost of the raw glass. That ratio was driven almost entirely by grinding and

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:32.360
fitting labor. The molded hydrogel route eliminated the grinding step, the polishing step,

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:38.140
and most of the fitting appointments. It replaced a rigid material that required individual curvature

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:43.200
matching with a flexible one whose base curve could be standardized across many eyes.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:49.840
Each of those changes removed a distinct cost center. Machine time disappeared, skilled operator

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.980
hours disappeared, scrapped from over grinding disappeared. The carrying cost of keeping many unique

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:01.820
blanks in inventory disappeared. The first concrete move was to treat the lens as a formed chemical

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:07.620
object rather than a machined mechanical one. Liquid monomers are injected into a pair of

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:13.560
precision plastic molds that already encode the front and back surfaces. Heat or ultraviolet light

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:19.560
triggers polymerization in place. This locks the optical power and base curve without any cutting.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.560
After demolding, the soft lens is hydrated in saline so it swells to its final dimensions.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:31.280
It becomes pliable enough to drape over the cornea. Because the mold defines the shape,

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:36.500
the same production line can switch between prescriptions simply by changing the mold inserts.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.300
No new grinding program or operator skill is required for each power.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.600
The second move was to accept a shorter replacement interval. Instead of making one durable lens

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:53.460
that must last years and therefore demands perfect individual fit, the process produces many

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:59.760
identical thin lenses that are discarded after days or weeks. This removes the need for the thick,

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:04.600
high modulus material that once resisted flexing and tearing during repeated handling.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:11.820
The third move addressed oxygen delivery directly. The polymer chemistry itself was tuned so the water

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:18.420
content and the polymer network allows sufficient oxygen flux. A requirement that rigid glass could never

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:24.320
meet no matter how thin it was ground. High volume polymerization lines now produce lenses whose

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.600
material cost is measured in pennies. The packaged sterilized product reaches the user at a price

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:36.780
low enough for daily disposability. The idiot index has fallen sharply because the dominant expenses

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:43.660
shifted from custom fabrication to repeatable chemical processing and packaging. What remains is the

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:50.200
cost of quality control, regulatory validation and the sealed blister packs that keep each lens sterile

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:56.680
until use. Those steps still sit well above the raw polymer floor. The redesign accepted a

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:02.640
shorter wear life in exchange for lower per lens cost and better oxygen delivery. That compromise

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.700
would have been impossible inside the rigid glass paradigm. The unsolved portion of the gap lies in

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:15.120
the final delivery system rather than the lens itself. Even after the polymer cost collapsed, the finished

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:21.560
lens must still pass through automated inspection for defects. It must pass through ethylene oxide or

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:27.600
steam sterilization. It must pass through placement into foil sealed blisters that maintain sterility

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:34.420
for months on a shelf. Those downstream operations set a new, lower but still visible floor beneath which

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:39.980
further price reduction becomes difficult without changing how lenses are distributed and prescribed.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:46.880
A lens whose price once reflected hours of hand grinding on glass now reflects the economics of pouring

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:53.320
a liquid into a mold and letting chemistry do the shaping. The same move, replacing removal of material

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:59.300
with controlled formation of material appears whenever a part's cost is dominated by legacy shaping

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:05.400
steps rather than the atoms required. Tomorrow the show returns with another concrete case or an

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:10.820
industry still waiting for the same question to be asked. That's first principles daily for today.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:16.780
If this changed how you see what things cost, share it with someone who depreciated and subscribe

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:23.820
wherever you listen. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, one example or one opportunity every day. See you tomorrow.

